<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brantas River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brantas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brantas River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, flowing in a great arc around the Kelud and Arjuno volcanoes to the sea at Surabaya. Its fertile valley was the heartland of East Javanese kingdoms from the 10th century, including Kahuripan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> built dams to control its floods, and its mouth sheltered the port of Surabaya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brantas-river.jpg" alt="Brantas River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NFarras · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brantas_River_in_Minggiran,_East_Java.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wijaya's political acumen, combined with the fertile rice plains of the <b>Brantas</b> valley and access to trade routes across the Java Sea, enabled the new state to secure both agricultural and commercial resources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Surabaya, the strongest rival, surrendered in 1625 after a long siege during which, according to Dutch reports, the besiegers cut off its water supply by damming the <b>Brantas</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…burden of temple-building on peasants who drifted east; Coedès and de Casparis saw a retreat from the Buddhist Shailendras or from Srivijayan invasion, and de Casparis noted that the <b>Brantas</b> valley gave access to the north-coast ports and the Maluku spice trade which the isolated Kedu plain lacked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Surabaya, at the mouth of the <b>Brantas River</b>, grew as a Muslim port in the late 15th century as Majapahit declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-surabaya">Duchy of Surabaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Janggala arose around 1045 from Airlangga's partition of Kahuripan, which gave one son the eastern lands around the <b>Brantas</b> delta, the old capital Kahuripan and the coastal ports near modern Surabaya and Sidoarjo.</blockquote><a href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a></li><li><blockquote>He built dams on the <b>Brantas River</b>, encouraged trade through the port of Hujung Galuh, and patronised Hindu and Buddhist clergy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li></ul></section>
